      "clusterId": "hackernews:50062237",
      "url": "https://avclub.com/epic-games-disney-ai-delay",
      "sourceTitle": "Epic Games tells employees to use AI to get Disney game done",
      "source": "hackernews",
      "ok": true,
      "title": "Epic Games tells employees to use AI to get Disney game done",
      "text": "It seems as though Epic Games has gotten itself in a bit of a pickle. In March, the gaming company laid off 1,000 employees after a reported “downturn in Fortnite engagement” forced them to cut costs. But, wouldn’t you know it, those employees were actually pretty crucial in making the projects that could have helped the developer boost its engagement. The company is working on a Disney project, which will feature characters from Marvel and Star Wars, which was set to be out in March. However, according to a new report in Bloomberg, the company underestimated how many people it would take to produce the game. Now, not only is the project delayed, but it’s an “AI first” project.\nPredictably, this news is not being received especially warmly within the Epic Games staff, which has still been working hard despite losing hundreds of their colleagues. Last week, when the powers that be in the company announced that there would be “No more hand-written code” and that the game will be “generated with full AI workflows,” employees were reportedly “stunned,” which many hoped the news was some kind of sick joke. However, a spokesperson from Epic confirmed the news, telling Bloomberg, “We’re embracing AI-accelerated development, led by Epic developers who are the visionaries and creative drivers for everything we do.”\nDisney didn’t offer a comment in Bloomberg‘s report, but it’s hard to imagine the company would be too opposed to this, given that less than a year ago it was gung-ho about adding user-generated AI content to its Disney+ app. Of course, Sora doesn’t exist anymore so that deal is no more, but it certainly set a precedent about what it was willing to do with its very valuable IP.",
      "imageUrl": "https://img.pastemagazine.com/wp-content/avuploads/2026/03/29134321/fortnite_metaverse_1920.jpg",
      "siteName": "AV Club"
    },
    {
      "clusterId": "hackernews:50065692",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13928",
      "sourceTitle": "AI models can provably hide arbitrary capabilities",
      "source": "hackernews",
      "ok": true,
      "title": "[2610.13928] AI Models Can Provably Hide Arbitrary Capabilities",
      "text": "Computer Science > Machine Learning\n\narXiv:2610.13928 (cs)\n\nTitle:AI Models Can Provably Hide Arbitrary Capabilities\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled AI Models Can Provably Hide Arbitrary Capabilities, by Andis Draguns and 3 other authors\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\nAbstract:Capability evaluations aim to surface dangerous model behaviors before deployment, but their reliability depends on the assumption that hidden capabilities can be elicited. We show this assumption does not hold under adversarial conditions by constructing backdoor attacks that embed encrypted neural networks within host model weights, decrypting and executing them only upon receiving a secret trigger. Using digital lockers, these hidden circuits remain provably hard to elicit and interpret under cryptographic assumptions, even given full access to model weights, extending prior work from hiding fixed strings to arbitrary computations. We implement our proof of concept constructions in PyTorch and evaluate elicitation through fine-tuning, weight noising, gradient-based trigger search, and latent adversarial training on a small chemical reaction prediction task - a proxy for CBRN-relevant capabilities. Our results demonstrate resistance to these methods and, together with our cryptographic guarantees, reveal a limit of elicitation-based capability audits. We release our models to support the improvement of defenses.\n\nSubmission history From: Andis Draguns [view email]\n\n[v1] Fri, 9 Oct 2026 17:55:06 UTC (878 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\nAccess Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled AI Models Can Provably Hide Arbitrary Capabilities, by Andis Draguns and 3 other authors\nView PDF\nHTML (experimental)\nTeX Source\n\nCurrent browse context:\ncs.LG\n\nLoading...\n\nBibTeX formatted citation\n\nBookmark\n\nBibliographic Tools\n\nBibliographic and Citation Tools\n\nBibliographic Explorer Toggle\n\nCode, Data, Media\n\nCode, Data and Media Associated with this Article\n\nDemos\n\nRelated Papers\n\nRecommenders and Search Tools\n\nIArxiv recommender toggle\n\nAbout arXivLabs\n\narXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\narXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nBoth individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nHave an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? Learn more about arXivLabs.",
      "imageUrl": "https://arxiv.org/static/browse/0.3.4/images/arxiv-logo-fb.png",
      "byline": "[Submitted on 9 Oct 2026]",
      "siteName": "arXiv.org"
